(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("multi-cloud",{"multi-cloud/service-mapping-compute-storage-and-databases":{"html":"\u003cp>Engineers who know one cloud often assume the other works the same way. The names are similar, the defaults are not, and the differences cause most multi-cloud surprises.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Compute: EC2, Auto Scaling and Azure VMs, scale sets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Containers and Kubernetes: EKS and AKS\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Object, block and file storage\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Managed databases and messaging\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Where equivalents differ\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"compute\">Compute\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An EC2 instance maps to an Azure virtual machine, and an Auto Scaling group maps to a virtual machine scale set. Instance families, burst rules, spot and low priority behaviour, and the way disks attach all differ. Learn sizing names for both, and verify quotas in each region.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"containers\">Containers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>EKS and AKS are managed Kubernetes control planes. Node group handling, default networking plugins, identity integration and upgrade cadence differ. Keep workloads on standard Kubernetes APIs and treat the cloud specific parts, such as load balancers and storage classes, as a thin layer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"storage\">Storage\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>S3 maps to Azure Blob Storage, EBS to managed disks and EFS to Azure Files. Consistency, tiering names, replication options and request pricing differ. Check durability and redundancy settings explicitly, such as zone redundant or geo redundant storage.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"databases-and-messaging\">Databases and messaging\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>RDS maps to Azure Database services, DynamoDB to Cosmos DB, SQS and SNS to Service Bus and Event Grid, and Kinesis to Event Hubs. APIs and guarantees differ. For example, ordering, delivery and throughput limits are not interchangeable, so check each service documentation before assuming parity.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"where-equivalents-differ\">Where equivalents differ\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Look at limits, default encryption, backup retention, failover behaviour and billing units. A service with the same purpose can have different maximum sizes, different recovery behaviour and a different cost model. Keep a living mapping table with notes on the differences you find.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"aws&gt;map;map&gt;az;az&gt;app\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Service mapping: compute, storage and databases\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"aws\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">AWS service\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"map\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Mapping table\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"az\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Azure service\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"app\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Application\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, AWS service to Mapping table; 2, Mapping table to Azure service; 3, Azure service to Application.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"aws\">\u003cdt>AWS service \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> EC2, S3, RDS.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Assumed parity.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"map\">\u003cdt>Mapping table \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Notes on differences.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Out of date.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"az\">\u003cdt>Azure service \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> VMs, Blob, SQL.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Different default.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"app\">\u003cdt>Application \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Uses a thin layer.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Hard coded.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-queue-that-did-not-behave-like-the-other-cloud\">Incident: A queue that did not behave like the other cloud\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A team moved a worker from SQS to Service Bus and messages arrived twice.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"az servicebus queue show --resource-group rg-app --namespace-name sb-app --name jobs --query &quot;{lock:lockDuration,maxDelivery:maxDeliveryCount}&quot; -o json\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>az servicebus queue show --resource-group rg-app --namespace-name sb-app --name \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">jobs\u003c/span> --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;{lock:lockDuration,maxDelivery:maxDeliveryCount}&quot;\u003c/span> -o json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">{&quot;lock&quot;:&quot;PT1M&quot;,&quot;maxDelivery&quot;:10}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The worker took ninety seconds per message and the lock duration was one minute, so the message was delivered again while the first copy was still running. The team raised the lock duration, made the handler idempotent and recorded the difference between visibility timeout and message lock in the mapping table.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Assuming a similar name means the same behaviour\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring quotas and limits in the second cloud\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not checking default redundancy settings\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No mapping document\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rewriting everything instead of choosing a thin layer\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"compute","text":"Compute"},{"id":"containers","text":"Containers"},{"id":"storage","text":"Storage"},{"id":"databases-and-messaging","text":"Databases and messaging"},{"id":"where-equivalents-differ","text":"Where equivalents differ"},{"id":"incident-a-queue-that-did-not-behave-like-the-other-cloud","text":"Incident: A queue that did not behave like the other cloud"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Azure to AWS service comparison","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/architecture/aws-professional/services"},{"t":"AWS Well-Architected Framework","u":"https://aws.amazon.com/architecture/well-architected/"},{"t":"Azure Well-Architected Framework","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/well-architected/"}],"text":"Engineers who know one cloud often assume the other works the same way. The names are similar, the defaults are not, and the differences cause most multi-cloud surprises. What you will learn Compute: EC2, Auto Scaling and Azure VMs, scale sets Containers and Kubernetes: EKS and AKS Object, block and file storage Managed databases and messaging Where equivalents differ Compute An EC2 instance maps to an Azure virtual machine, and an Auto Scaling group maps to a virtual machine scale set. Instance families, burst rules, spot and low priority behaviour, and the way disks attach all differ. Learn sizing names for both, and verify quotas in each region. Containers EKS and AKS are managed Kubernetes control planes. Node group handling, default networking plugins, identity integration and upgrade cadence differ. Keep workloads on standard Kubernetes APIs and treat the cloud specific parts, such as load balancers and storage classes, as a thin layer. Storage S3 maps to Azure Blob Storage, EBS to managed disks and EFS to Azure Files. Consistency, tiering names, replication options and request pricing differ. Check durability and redundancy settings explicitly, such as zone redundant or geo redundant storage. Databases and messaging RDS maps to Azure Database services, DynamoDB to Cosmos DB, SQS and SNS to Service Bus and Event Grid, and Kinesis to Event Hubs. APIs and guarantees differ. For example, ordering, delivery and throughput limits are not interchangeable, so check each service documentation before assuming parity. Where equivalents differ Look at limits, default encryption, backup retention, failover behaviour and billing units. A service with the same purpose can have different maximum sizes, different recovery behaviour and a different cost model. Keep a living mapping table with notes on the differences you find. Service mapping: compute, storage and databases Flow in order: 1, AWS service to Mapping table; 2, Mapping table to Azure service; 3, Azure service to Application. Service Data store Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. AWS service Service What it does: EC2, S3, RDS. If it fails or is weak: Assumed parity. Mapping table Data store What it does: Notes on differences. If it fails or is weak: Out of date. Azure service Service What it does: VMs, Blob, SQL. If it fails or is weak: Different default. Application Step What it does: Uses a thin layer. If it fails or is weak: Hard coded. Incident: A queue that did not behave like the other cloud Incident A team moved a worker from SQS to Service Bus and messages arrived twice. $ az servicebus queue show --resource-group rg-app --namespace-name sb-app --name jobs --query \"{lock:lockDuration,maxDelivery:maxDeliveryCount}\" -o json {\"lock\":\"PT1M\",\"maxDelivery\":10} (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The worker took ninety seconds per message and the lock duration was one minute, so the message was delivered again while the first copy was still running. The team raised the lock duration, made the handler idempotent and recorded the difference between visibility timeout and message lock in the mapping table. Common mistakes Assuming a similar name means the same behaviour Ignoring quotas and limits in the second cloud Not checking default redundancy settings No mapping document Rewriting everything instead of choosing a thin layer"},"multi-cloud/networking-vpc-vnet-peering-and-hybrid-connectivity":{"html":"\u003cp>Networking is where multi-cloud designs succeed or fail. Overlapping addresses, missing routes and DNS gaps cost days of debugging.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Address planning without overlap\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>VPC and VNet building blocks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Peering, transit and hub and spoke\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Private endpoints and DNS\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cross cloud and on premises links\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"address-planning\">Address planning\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Choose non overlapping CIDR ranges for every network in every cloud and on premises before anything is built. Keep a register of allocations. Overlaps cannot be fixed by routing alone and often need address translation or a rebuild.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"building-blocks\">Building blocks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An AWS VPC has subnets in availability zones, route tables, security groups and network ACLs. An Azure VNet has subnets, route tables, network security groups and user defined routes. Security groups are stateful in both. Azure subnets span zones, while AWS subnets live in one zone.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"peering-and-transit\">Peering and transit\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>VPC peering and VNet peering connect two networks but are not transitive. For many networks use a hub: AWS Transit Gateway or Azure Virtual WAN and hub VNets with a firewall. Hub and spoke keeps routing and inspection in one place.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"private-endpoints-and-dns\">Private endpoints and DNS\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Private endpoints give a service a private address inside your network. They only work if DNS returns that address, so link private DNS zones to the right networks and forward queries between clouds. Most private endpoint failures are DNS failures.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cross-cloud-links\">Cross cloud links\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Connect clouds with site to site VPN for modest traffic, or private circuits through a provider for steady, high volume traffic. Plan bandwidth, latency, failover paths and egress costs, because data leaving a cloud is billed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"a&gt;hub;hub&gt;z;z&gt;dns\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Networking: VPC, VNet, peering and hybrid connectivity\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"a\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">AWS network\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"hub\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Hub and firewall\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"z\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Azure network\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dns\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Private DNS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, AWS network to Hub and firewall; 2, Hub and firewall to Azure network; 3, Azure network to Private DNS.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"a\">\u003cdt>AWS network \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> VPC and transit.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Overlap.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"hub\">\u003cdt>Hub and firewall \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Central routing.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Single path.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"z\">\u003cdt>Azure network \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> VNet and hub.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing route.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dns\">\u003cdt>Private DNS \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Resolves endpoints.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong zone.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-private-database-that-nobody-could-reach\">Incident: A private database that nobody could reach\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After moving to private endpoints, the application resolved the public address and timed out.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"nslookup orders-db.postgres.database.azure.com\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>nslookup orders-db.postgres.database.azure.com\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Name:    orders-db.postgres.database.azure.com\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Address: 20.50.14.9\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The private DNS zone was linked to the hub network but not to the spoke where the application ran, so the name resolved to the public address that the firewall blocked. Linking the zone to the spoke fixed it, and the team added a DNS check to the pipeline that creates private endpoints.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Overlapping address ranges\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Expecting peering to be transitive\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Private endpoints without private DNS\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A single link between clouds\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring egress costs\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"address-planning","text":"Address planning"},{"id":"building-blocks","text":"Building blocks"},{"id":"peering-and-transit","text":"Peering and transit"},{"id":"private-endpoints-and-dns","text":"Private endpoints and DNS"},{"id":"cross-cloud-links","text":"Cross cloud links"},{"id":"incident-a-private-database-that-nobody-could-reach","text":"Incident: A private database that nobody could reach"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"AWS Transit Gateway","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/tgw/what-is-transit-gateway.html"},{"t":"Azure Virtual WAN","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/virtual-wan/virtual-wan-about"},{"t":"Azure private endpoint DNS","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/private-link/private-endpoint-dns"}],"text":"Networking is where multi-cloud designs succeed or fail. Overlapping addresses, missing routes and DNS gaps cost days of debugging. What you will learn Address planning without overlap VPC and VNet building blocks Peering, transit and hub and spoke Private endpoints and DNS Cross cloud and on premises links Address planning Choose non overlapping CIDR ranges for every network in every cloud and on premises before anything is built. Keep a register of allocations. Overlaps cannot be fixed by routing alone and often need address translation or a rebuild. Building blocks An AWS VPC has subnets in availability zones, route tables, security groups and network ACLs. An Azure VNet has subnets, route tables, network security groups and user defined routes. Security groups are stateful in both. Azure subnets span zones, while AWS subnets live in one zone. Peering and transit VPC peering and VNet peering connect two networks but are not transitive. For many networks use a hub: AWS Transit Gateway or Azure Virtual WAN and hub VNets with a firewall. Hub and spoke keeps routing and inspection in one place. Private endpoints and DNS Private endpoints give a service a private address inside your network. They only work if DNS returns that address, so link private DNS zones to the right networks and forward queries between clouds. Most private endpoint failures are DNS failures. Cross cloud links Connect clouds with site to site VPN for modest traffic, or private circuits through a provider for steady, high volume traffic. Plan bandwidth, latency, failover paths and egress costs, because data leaving a cloud is billed. Networking: VPC, VNet, peering and hybrid connectivity Flow in order: 1, AWS network to Hub and firewall; 2, Hub and firewall to Azure network; 3, Azure network to Private DNS. Service Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. AWS network Service What it does: VPC and transit. If it fails or is weak: Overlap. Hub and firewall Step What it does: Central routing. If it fails or is weak: Single path. Azure network Service What it does: VNet and hub. If it fails or is weak: Missing route. Private DNS Step What it does: Resolves endpoints. If it fails or is weak: Wrong zone. Incident: A private database that nobody could reach Incident After moving to private endpoints, the application resolved the public address and timed out. $ nslookup orders-db.postgres.database.azure.com Name: orders-db.postgres.database.azure.com Address: 20.50.14.9 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The private DNS zone was linked to the hub network but not to the spoke where the application ran, so the name resolved to the public address that the firewall blocked. Linking the zone to the spoke fixed it, and the team added a DNS check to the pipeline that creates private endpoints. Common mistakes Overlapping address ranges Expecting peering to be transitive Private endpoints without private DNS A single link between clouds Ignoring egress costs"},"multi-cloud/identity-and-access-iam-entra-and-federation":{"html":"\u003cp>Identity is the new network edge. Two clouds with separate user lists and long lived keys double the risk. Federation keeps one source of truth.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>AWS IAM and Azure RBAC models compared\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>One identity provider and federation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Workload identity for pipelines and pods\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Least privilege and permission boundaries\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Access reviews and break glass\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"two-models\">Two models\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>AWS IAM attaches policies to users, roles and resources and evaluates allow and deny statements. Azure uses role assignments of a role definition to a principal at a scope such as management group, subscription, resource group or resource. Entra ID is the directory behind Azure and can also be the identity source for AWS.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"federation\">Federation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use a single identity provider with single sign on to both clouds. People sign in once, get short lived sessions and lose access in all places when they leave. Avoid creating separate local users in each cloud.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"workload-identity\">Workload identity\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Pipelines and pods should not store cloud keys. Use OpenID Connect trust: a CI job or a Kubernetes service account presents a signed token and receives short lived cloud credentials. In AWS this is a role with a web identity trust, and in Azure it is a federated credential on a managed identity or app registration.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"least-privilege\">Least privilege\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Grant the smallest actions on the smallest scope. Use permission boundaries or service control policies in AWS and Azure Policy with deny assignments to stop dangerous actions regardless of role. Review broad roles such as owner and administrator access first.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reviews-and-emergency-access\">Reviews and emergency access\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Review who has access on a schedule and remove what is unused. Keep a break glass account with strong controls, monitored use and a documented process, for the day the identity provider is unavailable.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"idp&gt;aws;idp&gt;az;az&gt;wl\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Identity and access: IAM, Entra ID and federation\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"idp\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Identity provider\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"aws\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">AWS roles\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"az\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Azure role assignments\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"wl\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Workload identity\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Identity provider to AWS roles; 2, Identity provider to Azure role assignments; 3, Azure role assignments to Workload identity.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"idp\">\u003cdt>Identity provider \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Single sign on.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Outage.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"aws\">\u003cdt>AWS roles \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Federated sessions.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Long lived keys.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"az\">\u003cdt>Azure role assignments \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Scoped roles.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Owner everywhere.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"wl\">\u003cdt>Workload identity \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Token exchange.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Keys in CI.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-leaked-access-key-in-a-build-log\">Incident: A leaked access key in a build log\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A pipeline stored a long lived cloud key and printed it in a failing step.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws iam list-access-keys --user-name ci-deploy --query &quot;AccessKeyMetadata[].[AccessKeyId,CreateDate,Status]&quot; --output text\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws iam list-access-keys --user-name ci-deploy --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;AccessKeyMetadata[].[AccessKeyId,CreateDate,Status]&quot;\u003c/span> --output text\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">AKIAEXAMPLEKEY123456\t2024-02-10T08:00:00+00:00\tActive\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A deploy user with a key that never expired had broad permissions, and the key appeared in a log that many people could read. The team deactivated it, replaced the pipeline credential with OIDC federation to a narrowly scoped role, and added a scan for keys in logs and repositories.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Long lived keys in pipelines\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Separate local users in each cloud\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Owner or administrator for everyone\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No review of unused access\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No break glass plan\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"two-models","text":"Two models"},{"id":"federation","text":"Federation"},{"id":"workload-identity","text":"Workload identity"},{"id":"least-privilege","text":"Least privilege"},{"id":"reviews-and-emergency-access","text":"Reviews and emergency access"},{"id":"incident-a-leaked-access-key-in-a-build-log","text":"Incident: A leaked access key in a build log"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"AWS IAM best practices","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html"},{"t":"Azure RBAC overview","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/role-based-access-control/overview"},{"t":"GitHub OIDC with cloud providers","u":"https://docs.github.com/actions/deployment/security-hardening-your-deployments/about-security-hardening-with-openid-connect"}],"text":"Identity is the new network edge. Two clouds with separate user lists and long lived keys double the risk. Federation keeps one source of truth. What you will learn AWS IAM and Azure RBAC models compared One identity provider and federation Workload identity for pipelines and pods Least privilege and permission boundaries Access reviews and break glass Two models AWS IAM attaches policies to users, roles and resources and evaluates allow and deny statements. Azure uses role assignments of a role definition to a principal at a scope such as management group, subscription, resource group or resource. Entra ID is the directory behind Azure and can also be the identity source for AWS. Federation Use a single identity provider with single sign on to both clouds. People sign in once, get short lived sessions and lose access in all places when they leave. Avoid creating separate local users in each cloud. Workload identity Pipelines and pods should not store cloud keys. Use OpenID Connect trust: a CI job or a Kubernetes service account presents a signed token and receives short lived cloud credentials. In AWS this is a role with a web identity trust, and in Azure it is a federated credential on a managed identity or app registration. Least privilege Grant the smallest actions on the smallest scope. Use permission boundaries or service control policies in AWS and Azure Policy with deny assignments to stop dangerous actions regardless of role. Review broad roles such as owner and administrator access first. Reviews and emergency access Review who has access on a schedule and remove what is unused. Keep a break glass account with strong controls, monitored use and a documented process, for the day the identity provider is unavailable. Identity and access: IAM, Entra ID and federation Flow in order: 1, Identity provider to AWS roles; 2, Identity provider to Azure role assignments; 3, Azure role assignments to Workload identity. Security Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Identity provider Security What it does: Single sign on. If it fails or is weak: Outage. AWS roles Security What it does: Federated sessions. If it fails or is weak: Long lived keys. Azure role assignments Security What it does: Scoped roles. If it fails or is weak: Owner everywhere. Workload identity Security What it does: Token exchange. If it fails or is weak: Keys in CI. Incident: A leaked access key in a build log Incident A pipeline stored a long lived cloud key and printed it in a failing step. $ aws iam list-access-keys --user-name ci-deploy --query \"AccessKeyMetadata[].[AccessKeyId,CreateDate,Status]\" --output text AKIAEXAMPLEKEY123456 2024-02-10T08:00:00+00:00 Active (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) A deploy user with a key that never expired had broad permissions, and the key appeared in a log that many people could read. The team deactivated it, replaced the pipeline credential with OIDC federation to a narrowly scoped role, and added a scan for keys in logs and repositories. Common mistakes Long lived keys in pipelines Separate local users in each cloud Owner or administrator for everyone No review of unused access No break glass plan"},"multi-cloud/landing-zones-accounts-subscriptions-and-guardrails":{"html":"\u003cp>A landing zone is the prepared ground teams build on. When it is good, a new team gets a secure account in an hour. When it is missing, every team invents its own rules.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Hierarchy: organisation, accounts, subscriptions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Separating environments and workloads\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Guardrails: SCP, Azure Policy and tagging\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Logging, security and networking baselines\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Vending new accounts with code\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"hierarchy\">Hierarchy\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>AWS uses an organisation with organisational units and accounts. Azure uses management groups, subscriptions and resource groups. An account or subscription is a strong boundary for permissions, quotas, cost and blast radius, so use many of them rather than one big shared one.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"separation\">Separation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Separate production from non production and sensitive workloads from general ones, usually into different accounts or subscriptions. Put shared services such as networking, logging and identity in their own accounts managed by the platform team.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"guardrails\">Guardrails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Service control policies in AWS and Azure Policy with management group assignments set rules that no one in a child account can undo: allowed regions, required encryption, blocked public storage, mandatory tags. Prefer preventive rules for serious risks and detective rules for the rest.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"baselines\">Baselines\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Every new account gets the same baseline: central logging to a protected account, security services enabled, standard network connection, budget alerts and a default set of roles. Baselines are code, versioned and reviewed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"account-vending\">Account vending\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Teams request an account through a pull request or a portal. Automation creates it, applies the baseline, connects the network and assigns the team. The goal is a short time to a safe account, with no manual steps.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"org&gt;pol;pol&gt;base;base&gt;vend\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Landing zones: accounts, subscriptions and guardrails\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"org\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Organisation\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pol\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Guardrail policies\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"base\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Account baseline\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"vend\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Account vending\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Organisation to Guardrail policies; 2, Guardrail policies to Account baseline; 3, Account baseline to Account vending.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"org\">\u003cdt>Organisation \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> OUs and groups.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Flat.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pol\">\u003cdt>Guardrail policies \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Preventive rules.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Too loose.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"base\">\u003cdt>Account baseline \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Logging and network.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Manual.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"vend\">\u003cdt>Account vending \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Code driven.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Tickets.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-public-storage-bucket-in-a-new-account\">Incident: A public storage bucket in a new account\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A team created a public bucket in an account that had no guardrail policy.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws s3api get-public-access-block --bucket team-exports\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws s3api get-public-access-block --bucket team-exports\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">An error occurred (NoSuchPublicAccessBlockConfiguration) when calling the GetPublicAccessBlock operation\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The new account was created by hand and missed the baseline that blocks public access, so a bucket with customer exports was readable from the internet for a week. The platform team applied an organisation wide policy that denies public buckets, moved account creation to an automated pipeline and added a detective rule for public exposure.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>One shared account for everything\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Guardrails that only warn\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Manual account creation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No central logging account\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No budget alerts per account\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"hierarchy","text":"Hierarchy"},{"id":"separation","text":"Separation"},{"id":"guardrails","text":"Guardrails"},{"id":"baselines","text":"Baselines"},{"id":"account-vending","text":"Account vending"},{"id":"incident-a-public-storage-bucket-in-a-new-account","text":"Incident: A public storage bucket in a new account"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"AWS Organizations","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/organizations/latest/userguide/"},{"t":"Azure landing zones","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/cloud-adoption-framework/ready/landing-zone/"},{"t":"AWS IAM best practices","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html"}],"text":"A landing zone is the prepared ground teams build on. When it is good, a new team gets a secure account in an hour. When it is missing, every team invents its own rules. What you will learn Hierarchy: organisation, accounts, subscriptions Separating environments and workloads Guardrails: SCP, Azure Policy and tagging Logging, security and networking baselines Vending new accounts with code Hierarchy AWS uses an organisation with organisational units and accounts. Azure uses management groups, subscriptions and resource groups. An account or subscription is a strong boundary for permissions, quotas, cost and blast radius, so use many of them rather than one big shared one. Separation Separate production from non production and sensitive workloads from general ones, usually into different accounts or subscriptions. Put shared services such as networking, logging and identity in their own accounts managed by the platform team. Guardrails Service control policies in AWS and Azure Policy with management group assignments set rules that no one in a child account can undo: allowed regions, required encryption, blocked public storage, mandatory tags. Prefer preventive rules for serious risks and detective rules for the rest. Baselines Every new account gets the same baseline: central logging to a protected account, security services enabled, standard network connection, budget alerts and a default set of roles. Baselines are code, versioned and reviewed. Account vending Teams request an account through a pull request or a portal. Automation creates it, applies the baseline, connects the network and assigns the team. The goal is a short time to a safe account, with no manual steps. Landing zones: accounts, subscriptions and guardrails Flow in order: 1, Organisation to Guardrail policies; 2, Guardrail policies to Account baseline; 3, Account baseline to Account vending. Service Security Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Organisation Service What it does: OUs and groups. If it fails or is weak: Flat. Guardrail policies Security What it does: Preventive rules. If it fails or is weak: Too loose. Account baseline Step What it does: Logging and network. If it fails or is weak: Manual. Account vending Step What it does: Code driven. If it fails or is weak: Tickets. Incident: A public storage bucket in a new account Incident A team created a public bucket in an account that had no guardrail policy. $ aws s3api get-public-access-block --bucket team-exports An error occurred (NoSuchPublicAccessBlockConfiguration) when calling the GetPublicAccessBlock operation (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The new account was created by hand and missed the baseline that blocks public access, so a bucket with customer exports was readable from the internet for a week. The platform team applied an organisation wide policy that denies public buckets, moved account creation to an automated pipeline and added a detective rule for public exposure. Common mistakes One shared account for everything Guardrails that only warn Manual account creation No central logging account No budget alerts per account"},"multi-cloud/portable-infrastructure-and-avoiding-lock-in":{"html":"\u003cp>Complete portability is expensive and rarely needed. Choose a few seams where moving would be possible, and use cloud services without guilt everywhere else.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Lock in is a cost, not a sin\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Portable layers: containers, Kubernetes, open standards\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Terraform across clouds: shared patterns, separate modules\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Data gravity and egress cost\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A decision record for each dependency\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"lock-in-as-a-cost\">Lock in as a cost\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Every managed service saves effort now and costs flexibility later. Measure both. A queue that could be swapped in a week is cheap lock in. A proprietary database used by forty services is expensive lock in. Decide on purpose, not by accident.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"portable-layers\">Portable layers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Containers and Kubernetes run in both clouds with the same manifests. Open protocols such as PostgreSQL, Kafka and OpenTelemetry keep data and telemetry movable. Keep application code free of cloud SDK calls by hiding them behind a small interface.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"terraform\">Terraform\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A single Terraform language does not make the resources portable, because AWS and Azure resources are different. Share structure, naming, tagging and testing patterns, and keep one module per capability per cloud behind a common set of inputs. Pretending an S3 bucket and an Azure storage account are the same resource usually creates a bad abstraction.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"data-gravity\">Data gravity\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Data is the hardest thing to move. Large datasets take time and money to copy out, and applications that read them become tied to their location. Place compute near the data and move results, not raw data, between clouds.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"decision-records\">Decision records\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>For every significant cloud specific service write a short record: why chosen, the exit path and the estimated cost to leave. Review them each year. When the exit path is unknown, that is the finding.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"app&gt;k8s;k8s&gt;data;data&gt;adr\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Portable infrastructure and avoiding lock in\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"app\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Application code\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"k8s\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Kubernetes layer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"data\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Open data formats\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"adr\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Decision records\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Application code to Kubernetes layer; 2, Kubernetes layer to Open data formats; 3, Open data formats to Decision records.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"app\">\u003cdt>Application code \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Small interface.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> SDK calls everywhere.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"k8s\">\u003cdt>Kubernetes layer \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Same manifests.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Cloud specific CRDs.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"data\">\u003cdt>Open data formats \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Postgres, Parquet.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Proprietary.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"adr\">\u003cdt>Decision records \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Exit path noted.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not written.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-migration-estimate-that-was-off-by-months\">Incident: A migration estimate that was off by months\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Moving a service between clouds revealed hundreds of calls to a proprietary API.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"grep -r --include=*.py -l &quot;boto3&quot; src | wc -l\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -r --include=*.py -l \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;boto3&quot;\u003c/span> src | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">212\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Cloud SDK calls were spread through 212 files, so moving the storage layer meant touching nearly every service. The team introduced a small storage interface, moved new code to it first and recorded the exit cost for each proprietary service in a decision record.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Treating all lock in as bad\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Hiding two different resources behind one fake abstraction\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cloud SDK calls scattered everywhere\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring egress costs and data gravity\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No exit path for key services\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"lock-in-as-a-cost","text":"Lock in as a cost"},{"id":"portable-layers","text":"Portable layers"},{"id":"terraform","text":"Terraform"},{"id":"data-gravity","text":"Data gravity"},{"id":"decision-records","text":"Decision records"},{"id":"incident-a-migration-estimate-that-was-off-by-months","text":"Incident: A migration estimate that was off by months"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Terraform AWS provider","u":"https://github.com/hashicorp/terraform-provider-aws"},{"t":"Azure to AWS service comparison","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/architecture/aws-professional/services"},{"t":"AWS Well-Architected Framework","u":"https://aws.amazon.com/architecture/well-architected/"}],"text":"Complete portability is expensive and rarely needed. Choose a few seams where moving would be possible, and use cloud services without guilt everywhere else. What you will learn Lock in is a cost, not a sin Portable layers: containers, Kubernetes, open standards Terraform across clouds: shared patterns, separate modules Data gravity and egress cost A decision record for each dependency Lock in as a cost Every managed service saves effort now and costs flexibility later. Measure both. A queue that could be swapped in a week is cheap lock in. A proprietary database used by forty services is expensive lock in. Decide on purpose, not by accident. Portable layers Containers and Kubernetes run in both clouds with the same manifests. Open protocols such as PostgreSQL, Kafka and OpenTelemetry keep data and telemetry movable. Keep application code free of cloud SDK calls by hiding them behind a small interface. Terraform A single Terraform language does not make the resources portable, because AWS and Azure resources are different. Share structure, naming, tagging and testing patterns, and keep one module per capability per cloud behind a common set of inputs. Pretending an S3 bucket and an Azure storage account are the same resource usually creates a bad abstraction. Data gravity Data is the hardest thing to move. Large datasets take time and money to copy out, and applications that read them become tied to their location. Place compute near the data and move results, not raw data, between clouds. Decision records For every significant cloud specific service write a short record: why chosen, the exit path and the estimated cost to leave. Review them each year. When the exit path is unknown, that is the finding. Portable infrastructure and avoiding lock in Flow in order: 1, Application code to Kubernetes layer; 2, Kubernetes layer to Open data formats; 3, Open data formats to Decision records. Step Service Data store Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Application code Step What it does: Small interface. If it fails or is weak: SDK calls everywhere. Kubernetes layer Service What it does: Same manifests. If it fails or is weak: Cloud specific CRDs. Open data formats Data store What it does: Postgres, Parquet. If it fails or is weak: Proprietary. Decision records Observability What it does: Exit path noted. If it fails or is weak: Not written. Incident: A migration estimate that was off by months Incident Moving a service between clouds revealed hundreds of calls to a proprietary API. $ grep -r --include=*.py -l \"boto3\" src | wc -l 212 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) Cloud SDK calls were spread through 212 files, so moving the storage layer meant touching nearly every service. The team introduced a small storage interface, moved new code to it first and recorded the exit cost for each proprietary service in a decision record. Common mistakes Treating all lock in as bad Hiding two different resources behind one fake abstraction Cloud SDK calls scattered everywhere Ignoring egress costs and data gravity No exit path for key services"},"multi-cloud/multi-cloud-reliability-cost-and-exit-plans":{"html":"\u003cp>A second cloud adds failure modes of its own. It helps when the design is simple, tested and justified by a real risk, not by a diagram.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Active active, active passive and cloud as failure domain\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The complexity bill of two clouds\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Comparing cost fairly: egress, support and skills\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Observability and operations across clouds\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Exit and failover rehearsals\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"patterns\">Patterns\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Most reliability needs are met inside one cloud with several zones or regions. Active passive across clouds uses a warm standby and a failover process. Active active spreads traffic over both, but needs data replication, consistent identity and global traffic steering. Choose the simplest pattern that meets the recovery targets.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"complexity-bill\">Complexity bill\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Two clouds mean two sets of tools, skills, security controls and on call knowledge. Data replication across clouds adds lag and cost. The extra failure modes can exceed the failure you were avoiding. Write down the specific risk you are reducing and how often it actually happens.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"fair-cost-comparison\">Fair cost comparison\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Compare compute, storage and request prices, but also egress, support plans, committed discounts, engineering time and the cost of running two stacks. A cheaper unit price can lose once data transfer and duplicate tooling are counted. Tag everything and report cost per service in a shared model.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"operations\">Operations\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use one observability stack for both clouds with consistent labels for cloud, region and account. Standardise on OpenTelemetry, shared dashboards and one on call process. Differences in provider status pages and incident notices should be tracked in the same place.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rehearsals\">Rehearsals\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A failover plan that has never run is only a guess. Run game days that shift traffic, restore from backup in the other cloud and rebuild from code. Record how long it took and what broke. Keep an exit plan that lists data, dependencies, order and a realistic timeline.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"risk&gt;pat;pat&gt;obs;obs&gt;reh\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Multi-cloud reliability, cost and exit plans\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"risk\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Named risk\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pat\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Chosen pattern\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"obs\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Shared observability\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"reh\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Rehearsal\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Named risk to Chosen pattern; 2, Chosen pattern to Shared observability; 3, Shared observability to Rehearsal.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"risk\">\u003cdt>Named risk \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> What are we avoiding.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Vague.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pat\">\u003cdt>Chosen pattern \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Simplest that works.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Too complex.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"obs\">\u003cdt>Shared observability \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> One view.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Two tools.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"reh\">\u003cdt>Rehearsal \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Game day.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Never run.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-failover-that-took-nine-hours\">Incident: A failover that took nine hours\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>The second cloud copy of the database was a day old and the runbook had never been tested.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -h standby-db -c &quot;select now() - pg_last_xact_replay_timestamp() as lag&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -h standby-db -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;select now() - pg_last_xact_replay_timestamp() as lag&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> lag\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 23:41:07\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The cross cloud replica fell behind after a link change and nobody noticed, and the failover steps used names that no longer existed. In the exercise the team lost a day of data and needed nine hours to bring the service up. After the review they added lag alerts, updated the runbook from code and ran a game day each quarter.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A second cloud without a named risk\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Active active without data strategy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Comparing only unit prices\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Two observability stacks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A failover plan nobody has run\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"patterns","text":"Patterns"},{"id":"complexity-bill","text":"Complexity bill"},{"id":"fair-cost-comparison","text":"Fair cost comparison"},{"id":"operations","text":"Operations"},{"id":"rehearsals","text":"Rehearsals"},{"id":"incident-a-failover-that-took-nine-hours","text":"Incident: A failover that took nine hours"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"AWS Well-Architected Framework","u":"https://aws.amazon.com/architecture/well-architected/"},{"t":"Azure Well-Architected Framework","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/well-architected/"},{"t":"Azure Virtual WAN","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/virtual-wan/virtual-wan-about"}],"text":"A second cloud adds failure modes of its own. It helps when the design is simple, tested and justified by a real risk, not by a diagram. What you will learn Active active, active passive and cloud as failure domain The complexity bill of two clouds Comparing cost fairly: egress, support and skills Observability and operations across clouds Exit and failover rehearsals Patterns Most reliability needs are met inside one cloud with several zones or regions. Active passive across clouds uses a warm standby and a failover process. Active active spreads traffic over both, but needs data replication, consistent identity and global traffic steering. Choose the simplest pattern that meets the recovery targets. Complexity bill Two clouds mean two sets of tools, skills, security controls and on call knowledge. Data replication across clouds adds lag and cost. The extra failure modes can exceed the failure you were avoiding. Write down the specific risk you are reducing and how often it actually happens. Fair cost comparison Compare compute, storage and request prices, but also egress, support plans, committed discounts, engineering time and the cost of running two stacks. A cheaper unit price can lose once data transfer and duplicate tooling are counted. Tag everything and report cost per service in a shared model. Operations Use one observability stack for both clouds with consistent labels for cloud, region and account. Standardise on OpenTelemetry, shared dashboards and one on call process. Differences in provider status pages and incident notices should be tracked in the same place. Rehearsals A failover plan that has never run is only a guess. Run game days that shift traffic, restore from backup in the other cloud and rebuild from code. Record how long it took and what broke. Keep an exit plan that lists data, dependencies, order and a realistic timeline. Multi-cloud reliability, cost and exit plans Flow in order: 1, Named risk to Chosen pattern; 2, Chosen pattern to Shared observability; 3, Shared observability to Rehearsal. Outside party Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Named risk Outside party What it does: What are we avoiding. If it fails or is weak: Vague. Chosen pattern Step What it does: Simplest that works. If it fails or is weak: Too complex. Shared observability Observability What it does: One view. If it fails or is weak: Two tools. Rehearsal Observability What it does: Game day. If it fails or is weak: Never run. Incident: A failover that took nine hours Incident The second cloud copy of the database was a day old and the runbook had never been tested. $ psql -h standby-db -c \"select now() - pg_last_xact_replay_timestamp() as lag\" lag 23:41:07 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The cross cloud replica fell behind after a link change and nobody noticed, and the failover steps used names that no longer existed. In the exercise the team lost a day of data and needed nine hours to bring the service up. After the review they added lag alerts, updated the runbook from code and ran a game day each quarter. Common mistakes A second cloud without a named risk Active active without data strategy Comparing only unit prices Two observability stacks A failover plan nobody has run"}});